ebt_ebitda":false,"_score_net_debt_ebitda":45.7,"_gate_accruals":-0.04954318361873947,"_gp_accruals":true,"_score_accruals":43.1,"_gate_rev_volatility":0.10723011292615242,"_gp_rev_volatility":true,"_score_rev_volatility":83.6,"_gate_margin_vs_hist":-0.02780901288224641,"_gp_margin_vs_hist":true,"_score_margin_vs_hist":100.0,"_gate_piotroski":5,"_gp_piotroski":false,"_score_piotroski":55.6,"_gate_spread":0.12797631966126274,"_gp_spread":true,"_score_spread":64.0,"_gate_roic_consistency":0.31274743542413086,"_gp_roic_consistency":false,"_score_roic_consistency":47.9,"_gate_incr_roic":0.18359433158381064,"_gp_incr_roic":true,"_score_incr_roic":77.9,"_gate_margin_advantage":0.15965370770543302,"_gp_margin_advantage":true,"_score_margin_advantage":86.6,"_gate_pool_share":0.008466700824547146,"_gp_pool_share":true,"_score_pool_share":43.4,"_gate_rev_durability":0.07278488811885975,"_gp_rev_durability":true,"_score_rev_durability":61.4,"_gate_fcf_durability":-0.1964042944977793,"_gp_fcf_durability":false,"_score_fcf_durability":0.0,"_gate_margins":0.0007213780526365288,"_gp_margins":true,"_score_margins":50.7,"_gate_fund_growth":0.21425847662257502,"_gp_fund_growth":true,"_score_fund_growth":100.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.0732391129864217,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":92.5,"_gate_share_shrink":-0.05159676651134004,"_gp_share_shrink":true,"_score_share_shrink":100.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.009211411997792657,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":84.6,"_gate_insider_own":0.01254,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":8.4,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Eagle Materials Inc., through its subsidiaries, manufactures and sells heavy construction products and light building materials in the United States. The company operates in four segments: Cement, Concrete and Aggregates, Gypsum Wallboard, and Recycled Paperboard. It engages in the mining of limestone for the manufacture, production, distribution, and sale of portland cement, including Portland limestone cement; grinding and sale of slag; and mining of gypsum for the manufacture and sale of gypsum wallboards used to finish the interior walls and ceilings in residential, commercial, and industrial structures, as well as containerboard and lightweight packaging grades. The company is also involved in the manufacture and sale of recycled paperboard to the gypsum wallboard industry and other paperboard converters; sale of readymix concrete; and mining and sale of aggregates, such as crushed stone, sand, and gravel. Its products are used in commercial and residential construction; public construction projects to build, expand, and repair roads and highways; and repair and remodel activities. The company was formerly known as Centex Construction Products, Inc. and changed its name to Eagle Materials, Inc. in January 2004. Eagle Materials Inc. was founded in 1963 and is headquartered in Dallas, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Michael R. Haack | CEO, President & Director | Age 52 | Compensation: $3.2M (FY2026)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 2K people. Revenue per employee of $825K ranks in the above the sector median among Basic Materials peers, improving at 11%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $71K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $8K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10).","financial_summary":["Eagle Materials Inc is a mid-cap basic materials business with a $5.7B market cap on $2.3B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +2% over the past three years and +7% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 21% against a 9% cost of capital \u2014 a 13-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat, and 24% gross margins are typical of commodity economics where competitors can replicate the product cheaply.","The business throws off $197M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 9 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.45\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 1.9\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 13\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Management returns a meaningful 7.3% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 1.9\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Priced near or above estimated fair value, so the margin of safety has been bid away","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.06288342252443187},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 8.3% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Basic Materials tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Late-cycle dynamics increase the probability of a slowdown","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Commodity price volatility and cost-curve position \u2014 in a downturn only the lowest-cost producer has a business, and the rest carry the overhead.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Environmental remediation liability \u2014 tightening regulations on emissions, tailings, and water use create open-ended balance-sheet risk that grows with each legislative cycle.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Trade policy and tariff whiplash \u2014 materials producers are first-order casualties of protectionist swings, and margin gets squeezed from both input and output pricing.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Reinvestment rate supports 21% organic growth capacity \u2014 capital is compounding inside the business","Operating margins 16% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","30 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","ROIC sits in the top quartile of Basic Materials peers \u2014 the moat is wider than the neighbourhood"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Eagle Materials (NYSE:EXP) Stock Price Target Raised at Citigroup","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/06/eagle-materials-nyseexp-stock-price-target-raised-at-citigroup.html","date":"2026-11-06","timestamp":1794001730.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.511,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["analyst_action"]},{"title":"Is Eagle Materials (EXP) Cheap After Record Revenue And Softer EPS?","source":"finance.yahoo.com","link":"https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/eagle-materials-exp-cheap-record-151618346.html","date":"2026-11-06","timestamp":1793978178.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.883,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"Is Eagle Materials (EXP) Cheap After Record Revenue And Buybacks?","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/materials/nyse-exp/eagle-materials/news/is-eagle-materials-exp-cheap-after-record-revenue-and-buybac","date":"2026-11-06","timestamp":1793965281.944,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.866,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings","dividend"]},{"title":"Eagle Materials Inc (EXP) (Q2 2027) Earnings Call Highlights: Record Revenue Amid Margin ...","source":"finance.yahoo.com","link":"https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/eagle-materials-inc-exp-q2-190554516.html","date":"2026-11-05","timestamp":17